WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.890
<v Narrator>Another service is AWS Device Farm,</v>

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:04.660
and just need to know it at a high level.

3
00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.660
But the idea is that, say you are running a big application

4
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.640
that you published on the app store or the IOS store.

5
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.740
Then you want to test that application across many devices

6
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.980
to make sure it works on all these devices.

7
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.020
So Device Farm is going to be an application testing service

8
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.650
for your mobile and even your web application.

9
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.460
And the cool thing is that you're going to have this test

10
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.210
run across real browsers and real mobile devices,

11
00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:29.750
and all these tests are going to

12
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.380
be fully automated using a framework.

13
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.270
The idea is by testing across real devices

14
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.450
would be browsers or mobile devices,

15
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.510
you're going to be able to improve the quality

16
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.180
of the web and your mobile apps

17
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.080
by making sure it runs correctly

18
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.780
on all the devices you want.

19
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.570
And in case it doesn't run correctly,

20
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.680
then you can generate automatically videos and logs

21
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.310
to document the issues that have been encountered.

22
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.300
And then even better, you can remotely log-in

23
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.000
to devices for enhanced debugging.

24
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.960
So this is what device farm looks like,

25
00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.060
as you can see, it found some issues,

26
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.180
already you can see it very found some issues

27
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.610
on the Nexus 6 and an LG G Flex 2,

28
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.030
and it tells you what has been going wrong.

29
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.770
And so you can look into this failure, for these two devices

30
00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:17.100
and understand why the test has passed on say an SCG device

31
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.140
but hasn't passed on your Motorola or your LG device.

32
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:20.973
And that's it.

33
00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:23.170
That's the whole idea behind AWS Device Farm.

34
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.920
I will see you in the next lecture.

